      "question": "Something may have spiked in Transformer 1's oil at MAIN recently, and I'd like it checked properly rather than by eye. Screen acetylene, co and hydrogen between 2024-01-15T00:00:00.000000 and 2024-01-19T00:00:00.000000 for anomalous samples. For each channel, list any flagged timestamps with their values, and give the total number of anomalies per channel. Then tell me whether flagged points on different gases fall on the same timestamp. If acetylene and hydrogen are both flagged at the same timestamp, treat it as possible arcing and recommend an immediate confirmatory resample. If only co is flagged, treat it as possible paper involvement and recommend a routine retest. If nothing is flagged, say the window looks stable. Use a proper anomaly detection model, not a fixed threshold. If the detector can't run because a channel has fewer than 4 non-null samples, report that channel's min, max and mean instead, and flag any sample more than 50% above that channel's mean as a manual outlier.",
      "answer": "I couldn't run the anomaly detector, so I can't give you a model-based anomaly list or say whether this window is stable.\n\n**Why it didn't run:** each channel has 4 non-null samples in the window, so the \"fewer than 4 samples\" fallback you set doesn't apply. The block is the tooling. The detector only reads data from a file, and none of my tools can write the sensor readings to one. Passing an IoT reference or the data inline both failed. The catalog also has no ready-made anomaly detection models.\n\n**Raw readings (2024-01-15 to 2024-01-18, one sample per day), for reference only:**\n\n| Timestamp | Acetylene (ppm) | CO (ppm) | Hydrogen (ppm) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 2024-01-15T00:00:00 | 7 | 32 | 2845 |\n| 2024-01-16T00:00:00 | 6 | 30 | 2950 |\n| 2024-01-17T00:00:00 | 8 | 35 | 2800 |\n| 2024-01-18T00:00:00 | 7 | 33 | 2900 |\n| Min / Max / Mean | 6 / 8 / 7.0 | 30 / 35 / 32.5 | 2800 / 2950 / 2873.75 |\n\n- **Anomalies per channel (detector):** not available for acetylene, CO or hydrogen.\n- **Same-timestamp flags across gases:** can't be judged without detector output.\n- **Simple 50%-above-mean check:** nothing trips it. The largest deviations from each channel's mean are acetylene 8 (+14%, 2024-01-17), CO 35 (+8%, 2024-01-17) and hydrogen 2950 (+3%, 2024-01-16). This is the fixed-threshold kind of check you asked me not to rely on, so it is context, not a substitute.\n- **Recommendation:** none of your three rules (possible arcing, possible paper involvement, stable window) can be applied until the detector runs. Treat the result as pending, not as stable.\n\nThe run history has an earlier detector result for Transformer 1 on 4 samples that flagged the second sample (2024-01-16 if it covered this same window). It doesn't record which gas or date range it used, so I haven't counted it here.\n\nTo finish this properly, I need a CSV of these readings saved somewhere I can reach, or a tool that exports the IoT data to a file. With either, I'd run a local outlier detector (SubLOF) on each gas and then apply your rules.",
